<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  MojoMap pf loader — ruling N1 (2026-09-30). Installs to /Library/LaunchDaemons/.

  WHY A DAEMON AT ALL. /etc/pf.conf says in terms: "PF will not be automatically enabled". Apple's own
  /System/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.apple.pfctl.plist runs `pfctl -f /etc/pf.conf` at boot, which LOADS
  the ruleset but does not ENABLE pf. Without something calling `pfctl -E`, our anchor is parsed and
  then ignored. This daemon supplies the missing enable.

  MECHANISM CHOSEN, and why. /etc/pf.conf evaluates exactly one anchor path, "com.apple/*", and loads it
  from /etc/pf.anchors/com.apple. There is no second evaluated path to nest under, and writing our rules
  inside Apple's own anchor file would put them in a file the OS owns and rewrites. So install-pf.sh
  appends two lines to /etc/pf.conf:

      anchor "mojomap"
      load anchor "mojomap" from "/etc/pf.anchors/mojomap"

  keeping a timestamped backup. That is the minimal reversible change, and it survives any later
  `pfctl -f /etc/pf.conf` by another component — our rules come back with Apple's, rather than being
  flushed by the next reload. The alternative considered and rejected was a separate top-level conf
  loaded only by us: it would win at boot but would be silently dropped the moment anything reloaded
  /etc/pf.conf, which is the failure mode that leaves the ports open while the guard still reads
  "pf enabled".

  KNOWN CAVEAT, and the guard that catches it: a macOS update can replace /etc/pf.conf and remove our
  two lines. RunAtLoad re-applies on the next boot, and network-guard.sh check n4 compares the anchor's
  LOADED rules against scripts/network/pf.anchor, so a silently dropped anchor fails the ritual rather
  than passing unnoticed.

  -E increments pf's enable reference count rather than forcing pf on, so this cooperates with any other
  component that also enables pf instead of fighting it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.fomomojodojo.mojomap-pf</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/sh</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <!-- Load the full ruleset (Apple's anchor plus ours) and enable pf. -E is the enable; -f is the
         load. Both in one call so a partial state is not possible. -->
    <string>/sbin/pfctl -E -f /etc/pf.conf</string>
  </array>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <false/>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/var/log/mojomap-pf.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/var/log/mojomap-pf.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
